<%# SVG paint servers and reusable ornaments, defined exactly once per document.
    Expects: theme.

    They live here rather than inside whichever SVG happens to be first because the room can be
    drawn twice on one page (a page-wide layer plus the hero's cornice), and two <defs> blocks
    would mint duplicate ids - at which point which gradient wins is a question about document
    order, not about the design. A paint server is resolved by id across the whole document, so
    one hidden svg feeds every other one.

    What goes IN it belongs to the skin: the gilded hall needs a bead pattern, the rose bower
    needs a bloom, a leaf, a bud and a bow. Both define the same two gradient ids, which is the
    shared vocabulary every skin has to provide. %>
<svg class="throne-defs" width="0" height="0" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
  <defs>
    <%- include('skins/' + theme.theme + '/defs') %>
  </defs>
</svg>
